course: world
lesson: 32
title: The heart, blood and breathing
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every cell of your body needs oxygen to turn food into energy, and oxygen comes from the air.
explain: When you breathe in, air goes down your windpipe into two lungs, which are full of tiny air bags; there oxygen passes into the blood and carbon dioxide, a waste gas, passes out to be breathed away.
explain: The heart is a muscle about the size of your fist. It squeezes about 70 to 100 times a minute and pumps blood through tubes called blood vessels to every part of the body.
explain: Arteries carry blood away from the heart, veins bring it back, and tiny capillaries join them inside every part of the body.
explain: So blood is like a delivery service: it picks up oxygen in the lungs and food from the gut, drops them off at every cell, and carries the waste away.
explain: You can feel each squeeze of the heart as a pulse at your wrist or neck.
fact: All cells of the body need oxygen to get energy from food.
fact: In the lungs oxygen passes from the air into the blood; millions of tiny air bags give a huge surface for it to pass through.
fact: We breathe out carbon dioxide, a waste gas made by our cells.
fact: The heart is a muscle that pumps blood around the body.
fact: Arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins carry blood back to it.
fact: Capillaries are the tiniest blood vessels; they reach almost every cell.
fact: The pulse is the beat of the heart felt where an artery is near the skin.
fact: A child's heart beats about 70 to 100 times a minute at rest.
fact: When you run, your working muscles need more oxygen, so your heart beats faster and you breathe faster.
fact: Red blood cells carry oxygen; white blood cells fight germs.
fact: The diaphragm is a muscle under the lungs that helps us breathe in.
fact: Smoking damages the lungs and makes it harder for them to take in oxygen.
exercise: Why does the body need oxygen? | Cells use it to get energy from food | It keeps the bones hard and strong all day | It makes the blood thick so it does not leak | It helps the ears hear sounds clearly
exercise: Where does oxygen pass into the blood? | In the lungs | In the stomach | In the heart | In the kidneys
exercise: What gas do we breathe out as waste? | Carbon dioxide | Oxygen | Hydrogen | Pure nitrogen made in the body
exercise: What is the main job of the heart? | To pump blood around the body | To clean the air we breathe before the lungs | To think and feel | To digest food
exercise: Which blood vessels carry blood away from the heart? | Arteries | Veins | Nerves | Tendons
exercise: Which blood vessels bring blood back to the heart? | Veins | Arteries | Muscles | Windpipes
exercise: What are the tiniest blood vessels called? | Capillaries | Arteries | Lungs | Tendons
exercise: What do you feel when you take your pulse? | Each beat of the heart pushing blood | The air moving in and out of the lungs | Food being pushed along inside the gut | Nerves sending messages up to the brain
exercise: What do red blood cells carry? | Oxygen | Germs | Bone | Thoughts
exercise: What do white blood cells do? | Fight germs | Carry oxygen | Digest food | Make bones grow
exercise: How is blood like a delivery service? | It brings oxygen and food to cells and takes waste away | It carries air from the nose down to the stomach and back | It brings food from the lungs to the bones | It carries thoughts from the brain to the heart
exercise: About how big is your heart? | About the size of your fist | About the size of your head | About the size of a pea | About the size of a football
exercise: Which muscle under the lungs helps you breathe in? | The diaphragm | The biceps | The heart | The tongue
exercise: Why is smoking bad for the lungs? | It damages them so they take in less oxygen | It makes them grow too big | It fills them with extra oxygen | It turns the lungs into muscle
test: A boy holds his breath. Why can he not do it for long? | His cells need a steady supply of oxygen | His lungs would burst at once | His heart would stop beating at once | His blood would run out of water
test: Which is true? | Blood picks up oxygen in the lungs and carries it to the cells | The heart makes oxygen and sends it to the lungs | Veins carry blood away from the heart | We breathe out mostly oxygen and hardly any carbon dioxide at all
test: A nurse presses two fingers on your wrist and counts. What is she measuring? | How fast your heart beats | How deep you breathe | How strong your bones are | How hot your blood is
test: If the arteries to a leg were blocked, what would the leg lose first? | Its supply of oxygen | Its bones | Its skin colour forever | Its nerves only
test: Why do your lungs have millions of tiny air bags instead of one big empty bag? | Many tiny bags give a huge surface for oxygen to pass through | Tiny bags are lighter than one big bag | Big bags would make the voice too loud | Tiny bags keep germs warm
test: What carries carbon dioxide from your toes back to your lungs? | The blood in the veins | The air in the windpipe | The nerves in the leg | The food in the gut
test: Which pair is right? | Arteries go from the heart, veins go to the heart | Arteries go to the heart, veins go from the heart | Both only go to the heart | Both only go to the lungs
test: Why is the heart called a pump? | It squeezes to push blood along | It sucks air down into the lungs each time | It pushes food down to the stomach | It pumps thoughts up into the brain
test: A cut finger gets infected and white blood cells gather there. Why? | They fight the germs that got in | They carry oxygen to the nail | They make the finger grow back faster | They digest the dirt for food
test: why's my heart going mad after running?? | Working muscles need more oxygen, so the heart pumps blood faster | The heart gets scared when you run | Running shakes the heart loose | The heart is trying to cool down the legs
test: press my wrist n feel a thump, what is that even | The pulse, the heartbeat felt in an artery near the skin | A nerve in the wrist sending a quick message down to the hand | Air moving through a tube in the wrist | The bone in the wrist clicking
test: Mia says we breathe in oxygen and breathe out oxygen too, just used. right? | No, we breathe out carbon dioxide, a waste gas from our cells | Yes, the same oxygen goes in and out | Yes, used oxygen turns into steam | No, we breathe out only nitrogen
test: a video said veins carry blood away from the heart. that right? | No, veins bring blood back; arteries carry it away | Yes, veins carry blood away and arteries bring it back | Yes, veins and arteries both carry it away | No, veins carry only air around the body
exam: Identify the organ in which oxygen passes from the air into the blood. | The lungs | The heart | The stomach | The liver
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the arteries? | They convey blood away from the heart | They convey blood back to the heart from the body | They convey air down the windpipe to the lungs | They convey food from the mouth to the stomach
exam: Identify the function of the red blood cells. | The conveyance of oxygen | The combating of germs | The digestion of food | The transmission of nerve signals
exam: Identify the muscle beneath the lungs that assists inhalation. | The diaphragm | The biceps | The heart | The calf muscle
